27: The Scale Atlas
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste into another conversation window. No tables, no boxes.

What It Is
A provenance-aware, versioned, structured map of the characteristic scales in every scientific field. A characteristic scale is a value of a physical or abstract quantity at which the appropriate description of a system changes: the scale at which new degrees of freedom appear, the scale at which a symmetry breaks, the scale at which a new collective behaviour emerges, the scale at which a new approximation scheme becomes necessary. The Scale Atlas records each scale with its value, its source, its uncertainty, the description below the scale, the description above the scale, and the relations between scales. It reuses the STAG Schema Library (App 17), the Scale-Separation Record Calculator (App 4), the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20), the STAG Graph Viewer (App 5), the PROV-O Exporter (App 3), the Revision History Viewer (App 7), and the Numerical Verification Tool (App 22). It is for physicists, biologists, economists, philosophers of science, and anyone who works across scales or wants to know where the boundaries between descriptions lie.

Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of characteristic scales from users.

Store each scale with its value, its units, its source, its uncertainty, and its provenance.

Record the field and subfield of the scale.

Record the description below the scale, the description at the scale, and the description above the scale.

Record the criterion that fires at the scale: change of degrees of freedom, change of symmetry, change of approximation scheme, change of collective variables, or other.

Record the relations between scales: which scales are above, below, or adjacent to a given scale, and which scales are related in other ways.

Record the reference in which the scale is defined or measured.

Record the uncertainty in the scale value, in the form of a confidence interval or a standard deviation.

Record the date of the measurement or the definition.

Enforce provenance completeness: every deposit has a depositor, a date, a source, and a tool version.

Enforce versioning: every deposit has a version, and updates create new versions.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by scale ID, scale name, field, subfield, scale value, scale units, criterion, depositor, deposit date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the scale description and the notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by field, by subfield, by criterion, by year, by depositor, by order of magnitude.

Support exporting a scale as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing a scale from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface for depositing, browsing, and querying.

Support moderation: scales can be marked as pending, approved, or rejected.

Support linking a scale to a STAG annotation if the scale has one.

Support linking a scale to a numerical verification result if the scale value has been verified.

Support linking a scale to a Physics Knowledge Library work if the scale is defined in a deposited work.

Support the scale hierarchy: a visualisation of the scales as a hierarchy, from smallest to largest, with the relations between them.

Support the field map: a visualisation of the scales by field, showing where the scales cluster and where they are isolated.

Support the order-of-magnitude view: a visualisation of the scales by order of magnitude, showing the range that each field covers.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic: same inputs produce same outputs.

Fast: query a typical scale in under 100 milliseconds; aggregate over 10,000 scales in under one second.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 scales.

Versioned: the atlas records the STAG schema version, the scale schema version, and the atlas version.

Reproducible: the atlas records the provenance for every deposit.

Portable: works on Linux, macOS, and Windows.

Well-documented: API documentation, scale schema documentation, and user guide.

Input Specification
Input 1. Scale deposit. Required for create and update operations. A deposit object conforming to the scale schema.

Input 2. Files. Optional. One or more files (PDF, LaTeX, plain text, structured data) supporting the scale.

Input 3. Provenance record. Required for create and update operations. The depositor ID, the timestamp, the source, and the tool version.

Input 4. STAG annotation. Optional. A STAG record describing the scale as a scale-transition claim.

Input 5. Numerical verification result. Optional. A numerical verification result checking the reported scale value.

Input 6. Work reference. Optional. A reference to a work in the Physics Knowledge Library where the scale is defined.

Input 7. Query. Required for read operations. A query object with fields for scale ID, scale name, field, subfield, scale value, scale units, criterion, depositor, deposit date, and keywords.

Input 8. Aggregation query. Required for aggregation operations. A query object with fields for the aggregation dimension and the filter.

Input 9. Authentication credentials. Required for authenticated operations.

Input 10. Bulk import file. Optional. A file containing multiple scales in JSON or JSON Lines format.

Input 11. Bulk export query. Optional. A query object selecting the scales to export.

Output Specification
Output 1. Scale record. JSON. The scale as stored, including its value, source, uncertainty, and provenance.

Output 2. PROV-O serialization. Turtle or JSON-LD. The scale mapped to PROV-O.

Output 3. Turtle serialization. Turtle. The scale as an RDF graph.

Output 4. Query result. JSON. The list of scales matching the query, with pagination.

Output 5. Aggregation result. JSON. The counts by the aggregation dimension.

Output 6. Full-text search result. JSON. The list of scales matching the search query.

Output 7. Scale hierarchy. JSON or a rendered graph. The scales as a hierarchy, from smallest to largest.

Output 8. Field map. JSON or a rendered graph. The scales by field.

Output 9. Order-of-magnitude view. JSON or a rendered view. The scales by order of magnitude.

Output 10. Related scales. JSON. The scales related to a given one.

Output 11. Bulk export file. JSON or JSON Lines. The scales matching the bulk export query.

Output 12. Audit log entry. JSON. The operation, the scale ID, the depositor, the timestamp, and the result.

Output 13. API response. JSON. The result of the API operation, with HTTP status.

Output 14. GraphQL response. JSON. The result of the GraphQL query.

Output 15. Backup file. A database dump or a JSON archive.

Module-by-Module Specification
Module 1: Scale Schema
The scale schema defines the structure of a scale deposit. It has the following fields:

scale_id (string, required)

scale_name (string, required)

description (string, required)

field (string, required)

subfield (string, optional)

scale_value (number, required)

scale_units (string, required)

scale_uncertainty (object: type, lower, upper, standard_deviation)

scale_source (string, required)

scale_source_reference (string, required)

scale_criterion (enum: delta_dof, delta_sym, delta_app, delta_eft, delta_col, delta_bind, expert, other)

description_below (string, required)

description_at (string, required)

description_above (string, required)

order_of_magnitude (integer, optional)

related_scales (array of scale relations)

stag_annotation (object, optional)

numerical_verification_result (object, optional)

work_reference (string, optional)

deposit_date (ISO8601, required)

depositor_id (string, required)

depositor_affiliation (string, optional)

provenance (object: source, tool_version, notes)

keywords (array of strings, optional)

notes (string, optional)

moderation_status (enum: pending, approved, rejected; default pending)

version (integer; default 1)

supersedes (string, optional)

superseded_by (string, optional)

Module 2: Database Schema
The database has the following tables:

scale. Holds the scale records. Fields: scale_id (primary key), scale_name, description, field, subfield, scale_value, scale_units, scale_uncertainty, scale_source, scale_source_reference, scale_criterion, description_below, description_at, description_above, order_of_magnitude, deposit_date, depositor_id, depositor_affiliation, provenance, keywords, notes, moderation_status, version, supersedes, superseded_by, created_at, updated_at.

scale_relation. Holds the scale relations. Fields: relation_id (primary key), scale_id (foreign key to scale), related_scale_id (foreign key to scale), relation_type (above, below, adjacent, same_order, related, independent), relation_note, created_at.

stag_link. Holds the STAG links. Fields: link_id (primary key), scale_id (foreign key to scale), annotation_id, created_at.

verification_link. Holds the numerical verification links. Fields: link_id (primary key), scale_id (foreign key to scale), verification_id, created_at.

work_link. Holds the work links. Fields: link_id (primary key), scale_id (foreign key to scale), work_id, created_at.

provenance. Holds the provenance records. Fields: provenance_id (primary key), scale_id (foreign key to scale), depositor_id, depositor_affiliation, timestamp, source, tool_version, operation (create, update, delete), created_at.

audit_log. Holds the audit log entries. Fields: audit_id (primary key), operation, record_type, record_id, depositor_id, timestamp, result, details, created_at.

moderation. Holds the moderation records. Fields: moderation_id (primary key), scale_id (foreign key to scale), moderator_id, moderation_status, moderation_date, moderation_notes, created_at.

Module 3: Scale Loader
Reads a scale deposit from a JSON file or an API request. Validates it against the scale schema. Validates any STAG annotation against the STAG schema. Validates any numerical verification result against the verification schema. Rejects malformed deposits with an error.

Module 4: Scale Writer
Writes a scale deposit to the database. Inserts into the scale, scale_relation, stag_link, verification_link, work_link, provenance, and moderation tables. Enforces referential integrity.

Module 5: Scale Reader
Reads a scale deposit from the database. Joins the tables to reconstruct the full deposit. Returns the deposit as JSON.

Module 6: Scale Updater
Updates a scale deposit. Creates a new version. Inserts a new provenance record. Updates the scale table.

Module 7: Scale Deleter
Deletes a scale deposit. Marks it as deleted (soft delete) or removes it (hard delete, based on configuration). Inserts an audit log entry.

Module 8: Query Engine
Executes queries against the database. Supports queries by scale ID, scale name, field, subfield, scale value, scale units, criterion, depositor, deposit date, and keywords. Supports pagination.

Module 9: Full-Text Search Engine
Executes full-text search against the scale description and the notes. Uses the database's full-text search capabilities.

Module 10: Aggregation Engine
Executes aggregation queries. Supports counts by field, by subfield, by criterion, by year, by depositor, and by order of magnitude.

Module 11: Exporter
Exports a scale as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle. Uses the PROV-O exporter (App 3) for the PROV-O and Turtle formats.

Module 12: Importer
Imports a scale from JSON. Uses the scale loader and the scale writer.

Module 13: Bulk Import and Export
Imports multiple scales from a JSON or JSON Lines file. Exports multiple scales to a JSON or JSON Lines file.

Module 14: REST API
Provides the REST API. Endpoints:

GET /scales. List scales with pagination and filters.

GET /scales/{id}. Get a scale by ID.

POST /scales. Create a scale.

PUT /scales/{id}. Update a scale.

DELETE /scales/{id}. Delete a scale.

GET /scales/{id}/provo. Get a scale as PROV-O.

GET /scales/{id}/turtle. Get a scale as Turtle.

GET /scales/{id}/related. Get the scales related to this one.

GET /scales/{id}/above. Get the scales above this one.

GET /scales/{id}/below. Get the scales below this one.

GET /scales/{id}/same-order. Get the scales in the same order of magnitude.

GET /scales/{id}/stag. Get the STAG annotation linked to this scale.

GET /scales/{id}/verification. Get the numerical verification result linked to this scale.

GET /scales/{id}/work. Get the work linked to this scale.

GET /scales/by-field/{field}. Get the scales for a given field.

GET /scales/by-criterion/{criterion}. Get the scales for a given criterion.

POST /query. Execute a query.

POST /aggregate. Execute an aggregation query.

POST /search. Execute a full-text search.

GET /hierarchy. Get the scale hierarchy.

GET /field-map. Get the field map.

GET /order-of-magnitude. Get the order-of-magnitude view.

POST /bulk/import. Bulk import scales.

POST /bulk/export. Bulk export scales.

POST /moderate/{id}. Moderate a scale (approve or reject).

GET /audit. List audit log entries.

Module 15: GraphQL API
Provides the GraphQL API. The schema exposes the same entities as the REST API, with the ability to traverse the relationships between them (scale to related scales, scale to STAG annotation, scale to verification result, scale to work).

Module 16: Command-Line Interface
Provides the command-line interface for administration:

db init. Initialize the database.

db migrate. Run migrations.

db backup. Backup the database.

db restore. Restore the database.

db import. Import scales.

db export. Export scales.

db query. Execute a query.

db aggregate. Execute an aggregation query.

db moderate. Moderate a scale.

db audit. List audit log entries.

Module 17: Authentication and Authorization
Provides authentication (API keys, OAuth, or JWT) and authorization (role-based access control: reader, depositor, moderator, admin).

Module 18: Audit Logging
Logs every operation (create, update, delete, query, moderate) with the depositor, the timestamp, the result, and the details.

Module 19: Backup and Restore
Provides backup and restore. Backup produces a database dump or a JSON archive. Restore loads the backup.

Module 20: Web Interface
Provides a web interface for depositing, browsing, and querying. Displays scales, their values, their sources, their descriptions below and above, their related scales, their STAG annotations, and their verification results. Provides a search box, a filter panel, an aggregation view, a scale hierarchy, a field map, an order-of-magnitude view, and a deposit form.

Module 21: Moderation Workflow
Provides a moderation workflow. Scales are created with moderation_status = pending. A moderator can approve or reject a scale. The moderation status is recorded in the moderation table.

Module 22: Scale Hierarchy
Produces a visualisation of the scales as a hierarchy, from smallest to largest. Each scale is a node. The relations above and below are edges. The hierarchy shows the chain of descriptions from the smallest scale to the largest.

Module 23: Field Map
Produces a visualisation of the scales by field. Fields are axes or regions. Scales are points. The map shows where the scales cluster and where they are isolated.

Module 24: Order-of-Magnitude View
Produces a visualisation of the scales by order of magnitude. Each order of magnitude is a band. Scales are points within the bands. The view shows the range that each field covers and where fields overlap.

Module 25: Related-Scale Finder
Finds scales that are related to a given one. Uses the scale_relation table and the scale value to find scales that are above, below, adjacent, in the same order of magnitude, related, or independent.

Module 26: Migration Manager
Manages database migrations. Each scale schema version has a migration script. Migrations are applied in order.

